  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x>$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & ={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-4)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y={x}^{2}(x-4)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/bfba80e21f4a458af6c4111191908a9d45d7d8c05f237db293cdad71a451d943.png)\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x,$ the product ${x}^{2}(x-4)$ is positive exactly when $x-4>0,$ that is, when $x>4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval \n\n$$\nx>4.\n$$",
